Without regulation, genetic engineering could be used to create genetic disparities, widening the gap between social and economic classes.
The absence of ethical guidelines may allow genetic modifications to be used for non-therapeutic enhancements, leading to a new form of social stratification.
History shows that unregulated technologies often lead to exploitation, and genetic engineering is no exception in its potential for misuse.
Lack of oversight could result in the prioritization of profit over public good, further entrenching inequality.
Ethical concerns arise when the benefits of genetic engineering are accessible only to the wealthy, creating a divide between the privileged and the rest of society.